## Current behavior

With `@angular/router`, we currently have the following types of guards, which are fired in the shown order:
- first, the _activation guards_ (`CanActivate` / `CanActivateChild`) to check if a route may be activated.
- then, the _resolve guards_ (`Resolve`) to fetch data before the route is being activated.

There is also a `redirectTo` option to perform a _static_ redirect. However, to do a _dynamic_ redirect, e.g. based on fetched data, the options are very limited.

We can do redirect inside a resolve guard:
```typescript
const routes = [
  {
    path: 'example',
    resolve: {
      myData: MyDataResolverWithRedirect // does a relative redirect to either 'foo' or 'bar'
    },
    children: [
      { path: 'foo', component: FooComponent },
      { path: 'bar', component: BarComponent },
    ]
  }
];
```
See this in action: [example 1](https://stackblitz.com/edit/dynamic-redirect-angular-v1)

However, this construction does not work. When you deeplink to `/my-module/foo` the redirect will then be triggered as well. Also, the resolve guard will be triggered AGAIN when navigating away. Thus, it will for example lead us to `example/foo/foo`.

Luckily, we can achieve a better result by performing the dynamic redirect with a `CanActivate` guard on an empty child route:

```typescript
const routes = [
  {
    path: '',
    resolve: {
      myData: MyDataResolver 
    },
    children: [
      // does a relative redirect to either 'foo' or 'bar'
      { path: '', canActivate: [MyRedirectGuard]}, 

      { path: 'foo', component: FooComponent },
      { path: 'bar', component: BarComponent },
    ]
  }
];
```
See this in action: [example 2](https://stackblitz.com/edit/dynamic-redirect-angular-v2)

However, it feels very cumbersome to abuse a `CanActivate` guard for this, and due to [this issue](https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/25431), we can only use this "hack" in lazy-loaded modules.

Also, the data is initially loaded TWICE: in both `MyDataResolver` and in `MyRedirectGuard`. However, in the redirect guard, I cannot reuse `myData` because it is not yet resolved.

And, of course, redirecting inside the `OnInit` of a parent component is the worst solution of all (it breaks back  button navigation).

## Proposal: a `Redirect` guard
It feels like _dynamic_ redirects inside route configuration is not really possible in Angular Router. Both Resolve guards and CanActivate guards have their disadvantages.

My proposal is a new type of guard: Redirect Guards. 

* They should be run **after** CanActivate and Resolve, so they can (re)use resolved data from Resolve guards
* They MAY be used on component-less routes
* They can be asynchronous

```typescript
const routes = [
  {
    path: '',
    resolve: {
      myData: MyDataResolver 
    },
    children: [
      { path: '', redirect: [MyRedirectGuard]}, // <-- has access to myData!
      { path: 'foo', component: FooComponent },
      { path: 'bar', component: BarComponent },
    ]
  }
];
```

### Example imlementations

```typescript
class MySimpleRedirectGuard implements Redirect {
  redirect(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, state: RouterStateSnapshot): string {
    return `/foo`;
  }
}

class MySimpleRelativeRedirectGuard implements Redirect {
  redirect(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, state: RouterStateSnapshot): string {
    return `${state.uri}/foo`;
  }
}

class MyAsyncRedirectGuard implements Redirect {
  redirect(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, state: RouterStateSnapshot): Observable<string> {
    return this.performSomeAsyncTask.pipe(
      map(() => `${state.uri}/foo`;
    );
  }
}

class MyResolvedDataRedirectGuard implements Redirect {
  redirect(route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, state: RouterStateSnapshot): Observable<string> {
    return `${state.uri}/foo/${route.data['bar']}`;
  }
}
```
